course: dictionary
lesson: 13225
title: Gangway: one word, two jobs
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and gangway is one of them. The sentence around it tells you which meaning is right. «The sailors walked down the gangway onto the dock.» Ships, sailors and the dock are the clues: a gangway here is a bridge between the ship and the land.
explain: «The usher showed us along the gangway to our seats.» In a theatre, the gangway is the passage between rows of seats. The seats and the rows are the clues, so you know which meaning fits.
explain: Gangway can also be a shout. «Gangway! Coming through with the heavy box!» Here it means move out of the way, I'm coming through. Look for the exclamation mark and the heavy box: they show that someone is in a hurry and needs room to pass.
explain: Always check your meaning by putting it back into the sentence. If «move out of the way» makes sense when the box is coming, you have it right.
fact: A gangway is a movable bridge or ramp for walking between a ship and the land.
fact: In the UK, a gangway can be a passage between rows of seats in a theatre, bus or plane. In the US, this is called an aisle.
fact: «Gangway!» is an informal shout that means move out of the way, I'm coming through.
fact: Gangways must be kept clear so people can get out in an emergency.
exercise: «The sailors ran up the gangway to board the ship.» What is a gangway here? | A knot used to tie a rope | A tall pole that holds up the sails | A type of cloud over the sea | *A movable bridge between the ship and the land
exercise: Ana shouted, «Gangway!» as she carried the huge tray through the crowd. What does Gangway mean here? | Hello, it's lovely to see you | *Move out of the way, I'm coming through! | Stop and wait here, please | Let's all sit down quietly
exercise: «Please keep the gangway clear, so everyone can get out if there is a fire.» Why must the gangway be kept clear? | *So people can get out in an emergency | So the ship can sail faster | So the seats look neat and tidy | So the rain can drain away
test: «The usher walked slowly down the gangway, checking the tickets.» Where is the gangway in this sentence? | *A passage between rows of seats in a theatre | A bridge from a ship to the land | A rope on a sailing boat | A shop near the harbour
test: Sam shouted, «Gangway!» as he rolled the barrel past. What does Sam mean? | I really like barrels | Stop the barrel right now | *Please get out of my way, I'm coming through | Please come and join me
exam: «The crew lowered the gangway, and the passengers stepped off the ship.» Where were the passengers going? | Up to the top of the mast | Under the deck | Down into the sea | *Onto the land
